{0 Completion in Markdown links}

Suggest vault paths and fragments as the user types inside the destination of
a Markdown link or image — [\[label\](<cursor>)] — the counterpart of
{!page-"feature-completion"}, which covers wikilink brackets only.

Both link syntaxes resolve through {!Oystermark.Vault.Index.resolve} and both
are diagnosed ({!page-"feature-diagnostics"}) and followed
({!page-"feature-go-to-definition"}). Completion was the one place the two
diverged, and the divergence was an omission rather than a decision: a vault
that preferred Markdown links got no help writing the destination it would
later be told was broken.

Impl: {!Lsp_lib.Completion}, the same handler that serves
{!page-"feature-completion"} — one trigger detector added beside the wikilink
one, feeding the fragment suggestions that were already there.

{1 Activation}

The request is [textDocument/completion]. The [completionProvider] trigger
character set gains [(] and keeps [\[] and [#].

[(] is a poor trigger in isolation — it fires on ordinary prose — so the
handler answers with an empty list unless {!trigger_context} matches. The cost
of the extra trigger is one no-op round trip per typed parenthesis; the
alternative, waiting for an explicit invocation, is worse for the feature that
exists to spare typing.

{1:trigger_context Trigger context}

Completion activates when the cursor is inside the destination of an inline
link or image that is still open:

{ul
  {- [\[label\](<cursor>)] — the destination}
  {- [\[label\](note#<cursor>)] — the fragment}
  {- [!\[alt\](<cursor>)] — image destination, same behavior}
}

Detection is textual and single-line, matching {!Lsp_lib.Completion} for
wikilinks:

{ol
  {- scan back from the cursor for the nearest [(] on the line;}
  {- it must be immediately preceded by [\]] — otherwise this is an ordinary
     parenthesis and there are no completions;}
  {- the text between that [(] and the cursor is the {e prefix}. A [)] means
     the destination is already closed, and there are no completions;}
  {- whitespace in the prefix ends the destination — {b unless} the prefix
     opens with [<], the angle-bracket form {!destination_escaping} writes,
     where the destination runs to the [>]. Without this exception the
     feature would abandon a destination the moment it inserted one, and
     [\[a\](<my note.md>#<cursor>)] — a fragment in exactly the file the
     escaping exists to serve — would never complete;}
  {- the [!] before the [\[] of the label, if present, marks {e image
     context}.}
}

The label's own brackets are not required to be well-formed: [\[\](<cursor>)]
completes. The label text is not consulted.

{b Precedence.} Wikilink detection runs first. Inside
[\[\[note (draft)#<cursor>\]\]] the cursor is within wikilink brackets and the
[(] is part of a note name, so the wikilink handler answers and this one is
not consulted.

{1 Completion modes}

{2:path_completion Path completion}

When the prefix contains no [#], suggest vault paths.

Unlike {!page-"feature-completion".note_name_completion}, the suggestion is a
{e path with its extension}, not a shortest unambiguous name.

Not because a shorter name would fail to resolve — it would. Both syntaxes go
through {!Oystermark.Vault.Index.resolve}, which matches a bare unique
basename by subsequence like any wikilink, and appends [.md] to a target
containing no [.] at all. The reasons are that the appending rule tests for a
dot {e anywhere}, so a real path like [notes/v1.2/draft] is left unresolvable
by exactly the shortening a name-based suggestion would do; and that a
Markdown destination is read as a path by every other tool that will ever see
this file. What is offered is {e a} valid destination, the least surprising
one — not the only one that works.

{ul
  {- {b Markdown notes}: every [.md] file in the index. [label] is the
     vault-relative path {e with} its [.md] suffix, [detail] is the note's
     title (its first H1, when it has one), [kind] is
     [CompletionItemKind.File].}
  {- {b Assets}: every non-[.md] file the index walked. Same shape, no
     [detail].}
}

In image context, assets whose extension is one of the recognized image
extensions (see {!Lsp_lib.Link_collect.is_image_target}) sort first; notes are
still offered, because an image-syntax embed of a note is a transclusion and a
legitimate thing to write. Outside image context the order is reversed. Within
each group, order is alphabetical by label.

{2:destination_escaping Destination escaping}

A path containing a space is not a well-formed Markdown destination. Such an
item wraps the path in angle brackets — [<my note.md>] — which is the form
CommonMark defines and the parser accepts: [Match.link_destination] keeps the
brackets out of the destination it reports, so the resolver never sees them.

All three encodings would in fact resolve. Backslash escapes are removed from
the destination before it reaches us, and
{!Oystermark.Note.Link.Ref.percent_decode} runs on every Markdown
destination, so [my\ note.md] and [my%20note.md] resolve as readily as the
angle-bracketed form. The choice between them is therefore about {e reading},
not validity: angle brackets leave the path legible in the source, and this
text is read by people.

Paths containing [<], [>], or an unbalanced parenthesis are offered with those
characters backslash-escaped. A path that cannot be written safely at all is
still offered, unescaped, rather than hidden — the user can see what is wrong
where a silent omission would look like a missing file.

{2:fragment_completion Fragment completion}

When the prefix contains [#], the text before [#] is the destination path and
the text after it is the fragment prefix. An empty destination
([\[label\](#<cursor>)]) means the current file.

Suggestions are exactly those of
{!page-"feature-completion".fragment_completion} — headings, caret block ids,
and djot attribute ids, sharing one namespace — with one difference: a heading
item's [insertText] is the slug, and the slug is used {e as written by the
parser}, unescaped. Fragments are identifiers; they do not contain spaces.

If the destination does not resolve, no fragment suggestions are returned.

{1 Completion item shape}

{t
  | Field      | Path mode                     | Fragment mode         |
  |------------|-------------------------------|-----------------------|
  | label      | vault-relative path with extension | heading text or [^id] |
  | detail     | note title, for [.md] files   | —                     |
  | filterText | the path                      | slug or block id      |
  | textEdit   | the path, escaped per {!destination_escaping}, replacing the whole prefix | slug or [^blockid], replacing the fragment prefix |
  | kind       | File                          | Reference             |
}

{2:replace_range The replace range}

The item carries a {b [textEdit]} with an explicit range covering the prefix —
everything between the opening [(] (or the [#], in fragment mode) and the
cursor — rather than an [insertText] left to the client to place.

This is not a refinement. A client given [insertText] chooses what to replace
by its own word rules, and VS Code's word boundary excludes [/]: accepting
[notes/deep.md] against a typed [notes/] yields [notes/notes/deep.md]. Paths
make that the ordinary case rather than a corner one.

{b The wikilink handler had the same defect}, and it falls back to a full
relative path whenever a basename is ambiguous — precisely when the path
contains a [/]. Both are fixed: {!Lsp_lib.Completion.complete} returns one
[replace_from] offset for the whole response, whichever syntax asked, and
{!Lsp_lib.Server.completion} turns it into the range every item carries.

{2 Volume}

A vault with an asset folder can offer thousands of paths on a single [(].
Suggestions are capped, and the response is marked [isIncomplete] when the cap
bites, so the client re-asks as the user narrows rather than being handed the
vault. The cap is a constant in the implementation, not a setting: it exists to
keep a keystroke cheap, and a user has no way to know what value would.

{1:testing Testing}

{ul
  {- Trigger detection: a table over cursor positions covering each form
     above, the [(] not preceded by [\]] case, a closed destination, an
     already-spaced destination, and the wikilink-precedence case.}
  {- Path mode: a fixture vault with a nested note, an asset, and a name
     containing a space, asserting the offered set, the angle-bracket
     insertion, and the replace range on a prefix containing a [/].}
  {- Image context: asserting the ordering flip, not a filtered set.}
  {- Fragment mode: the same expectations as the wikilink fragment tests,
     driven through Markdown syntax.}
  {- Round-trip: for every offered item, inserting it and re-parsing yields a
     link that {!Oystermark.Vault.Index.resolve} resolves to the file the
     item came from. This is the obligation the escaping rules exist to meet,
     and it is the one worth stating as a property.}
}

All of the above are inline expect tests in {!Lsp_lib.Completion}. The wire
shape — a [CompletionList] rather than a bare array, and a [textEdit] rather
than an [insertText] — is checked by [scripts/smoke.py] against the built
binary, since in process the result is an OCaml value that cannot be
malformed.

{1 Limitations}

{ul
  {- {b Inline links only}. Reference links ([\[label\]\[ref\]]) and their
     definitions are not completed; they are a separate namespace, and the
     vault has no reference-definition index.}
  {- {b Single-line detection}. A destination split across lines is not
     recognized, matching the wikilink handler.}
  {- {b No title completion}. The optional ["title"] after the destination
     is out of scope.}
  {- {b External URLs} are never suggested; there is nothing to suggest them
     from.}
  {- {b The empty destination is a resolved link.} [\[label\]()] — the state
     this feature triggers in — is given the target [().md] by
     {!Oystermark.Note.Link.Ref.of_cmark_reference}, following Obsidian, so
     diagnostics report a half-typed link as unresolved rather than ignoring
     it. Completion is unaffected; the noise belongs to
     {!page-"feature-diagnostics"} and should be settled there.}
  {- Positions and edits follow {!page-"feature-utf16-positions"}.}
}
